  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{6}-4{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}-4$ at the point where $x=-1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=({x}^{2}-1{)}^{3}+3.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is equal to ${f}^{\u2032}(-1).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{6}-4{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =6{x}^{5}-4(4{x}^{3})+8(2x)-0 \\\\  & =6{x}^{5}-16{x}^{3}+16x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=-1$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(-1) & =6(-1{)}^{5}-16(-1{)}^{3}+16(-1) \\\\  & =-6+16-16 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}(-1)=-6.\n$$",
